```text
Address Issue 08 — Agree the section argument and provisional page budget for the six-page PNAS course report.

Work in C:\Users\victo\Codex\Go_Nuts_repos. The working paper is pnas_report/paper_simple. Start by reading README.md and this Issue 08 scratch file. Then read final_research_question.md, paper_storyline.md, evidence_and_sources.md, project_requirements.md, tone_of_voice.md and scratch/REPOSITORY_CONTEXT.md. Read Issue 07's resolution and scratch/issue07_literature_review.md for the final citation decisions. Issues 01–07 are Resolved. Use their agreed outcomes as the starting point.

The agreed research question is “How does entry into the Premier League differ by player age, and how does the tone of Guardian coverage before and after Premier League signings differ by age?” Compare players aged 30+ and under 30 using completed-years age at transfer. H1 and Guardian analyses use separate event populations. Preserve the exact approved three-paragraph sample description and the complete H1 sensitivity passage in evidence_and_sources.md.

Begin by proposing a central descriptive claim and a result sequence in plain academic English. Explain how each result answers the question and why the transition to the next result is useful. Give one recommended outline and identify only the choices that still need the user's judgement. The first response should make the proposed argument and tradeoffs reviewable before recording agreement.

Use the following fixed evidence and decisions.

- H1 uses the confirmed restricted 2012/13–2024/25 outputs, linked from pnas_report/reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/provenance/output_parity_report.md. It includes 16,636 events involving 4,602 players. Joining rates are 12.14% for 30+ and 14.56% for under 30, a gap of −2.42 percentage points. The mean within-origin-club/season shuffled gap is −1.31 points and the one-sided p-value is 0.098. Use the agreed interpretation of limited evidence against this conditional null. The one-transfer-per-player result is conditional on one fixed selection. Preserve its approved motivation, results and limitation.
- The H1 all-eligible-transfers permutation plot is selected for Results. Carry the α = 0.20 whole-network backbone as a candidate context panel into Issue 09. Its colours use the saved Louvain partition. Keep the feeder analysis and primary seven-transfer figure in the repository. A brief feeder result is optional if space permits and requires the within-season route-null interpretation and unadjusted comparisons. Read the Issue 06 resolution if considering that prose.
- Include the agreed brief exploratory Leiden comparison. Both methods found five communities with broadly similar memberships and modularity scores of 0.448 and 0.446. Attribute faster runtime to Traag et al.'s original evaluation. Our saved comparison cannot rank their runtimes.
- Main Guardian tone analysis uses 820 sufficiently covered signings from a separate selection of 1,332 signings. RoBERTa measures how positive or negative selected published sentences read. The approved validation supports a useful but imperfect measure, with age-specific validity uncertain. H2's difference in changes is +0.0060 score units, p = 0.7558, with limited sensitivity to smaller differences. Preserve the approved wording and omit the historical between-age interval whose construction was unverified. Coverage volume includes all selected signings, including zero coverage, and shows greater attention for older signings in both periods. Retain the agreed four-instrument validation table in the space plan.
- H2b is exploratory and its inclusion remains with scratch/2026-11-21_15-45-35_15-explain-h2b-model-and-figure.scratch. Read it before proposing space for H2b. Show how the provisional budget accommodates inclusion or omission, with the choice explicitly pending Issue 15. Its adjusted tone-level comparison answers a different question from H2's comparison of changes.
- Use the approved CIES recruitment background and introduce our comparison independently. CIES's 10.6% older-recruit share and H1's 12.14% older-player joining rate measure different quantities. Use Dot Grau–Canter as a precedent for sentiment analysis across multiple newspaper sources including the Guardian. Use Monsees to motivate cultural, linguistic and source limits on generalisation. Ruijg–van Ophem is excluded. The exact selected passages are in the evidence inventory.
- Methods references are selected in Issue 07. Cite Hemerik–Goeman as the main permutation reference, with Phipson–Smyth and Winkler et al. as support. Preserve the agreed wording about repeated player transfers and articles shared across signings challenging exchangeability. The source inventory distinguishes H1's origin-club-by-season shuffle from H2's season-only shuffle. Sentiment and network papers support their procedures, with Guardian performance supported by this study's validation.

Work toward these deliverables through discussion.

1. A central claim proportionate to the descriptive findings and uncertainty, supported by a compact claim-to-evidence outline. Preserve the separate samples and observational interpretation.
2. The purpose and intended argument of Introduction, Results, Discussion and Methods, with the result order and transitions. Fit the agreed literature passages into that structure. Give the short answers to the question in Results and develop their meaning in Discussion.
3. A provisional page-budget table summing to at most six pages, including the 150-word Abstract, two- or three-sentence Significance Statement, all main sections, two or three figure slots, the validation table, author contributions and references. Use page fractions and optional approximate word counts as planning estimates. Allow room for captions and references, and distinguish text space from figure/table space. The compiled template will determine the final fit in later issues.
4. A clear handoff of pending decisions. Issue 09 settles figure selection and layout, Issue 10 develops reproducible Methods, Issue 11 develops the Discussion, and Issue 15 settles H2b. Link an existing issue when it already tracks a choice. Agree any new issue or change in work order with the user.

This session is for the section argument, result sequence and space plan. Keep full manuscript drafting for the agreed drafting stage. Use the existing saved outputs for evidence. Discuss proposed new data analysis or computational checks with the user before running them. Any approved script rerun must go to a new agent with fork_turns="none". Preserve unrelated working-tree changes.

Once the user agrees on choices, record them in paper_storyline.md and this scratch file, distinguishing agreed choices from proposals and unresolved dependencies. Resolve Issue 08 only after the user confirms satisfaction with the section argument, result order and provisional budget. Update the README checkpoint with the outcome and exact next action, and check that its status agrees with this issue.
